Let's talk about 2020 polling.

00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:04.149
Okay.

00:00:04.149 --> 00:00:08.370
It's been weeks since I talked about the Democratic
primary polling.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:12.640
Partially it's because I was gone all of last
week, but partially it's because we've not

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.400
been following it super closely at this point,
but we're now getting close to the start of

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.940
the Democratic primary debates.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.940
Those start next month.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:27.140
And at this point there are some really clear
and defined trends in 2020 Democratic primary

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:33.530
polling and the trends are for better or worse,
Joe Biden is leading but not by as much as

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.360
he was at the beginning of May.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.860
Uh, Bernie and Elizabeth Warren have solidified
their second and third place positions.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.969
Camilla Harris and peed booted judge or kind
of trading back and forth for the fourth place

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:48.450
position.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.719
And then they're just like, are a whole bunch
of candidates who can't even break one or

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:55.829
1% or 2% who are effectively done and have
no chance.

00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:59.490
So I want to go through this in order and
let's see kind of what we're left with at

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.490
the end.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:06.529
First of all, the Green Line, if you are watching
along with me here is Joe Biden's polling

00:01:06.529 --> 00:01:10.939
and I'll describe it in case you're listening
and not watching Joe Biden's polling hit a

00:01:10.939 --> 00:01:17.479
high average of more than 40% early in May
and it's now sort of cooled off to just under

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:18.490
35%.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:23.979
So Biden has lost somewhere around a six or
seven points depending on the poll you look

00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:26.210
at over the last three to four weeks.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:32.159
The question is, is Biden going to keep trailing
off or will he stick around 35%?

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:39.469
Uh, the progressive wing of the left is saying,
the more people know about Joe, the more his

00:01:39.469 --> 00:01:44.560
support is going to erode because people aren't
going to like what Joe Biden is about.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:45.990
That's a possibility.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:51.219
Um, it also could be that there was some initial
enthusiasm behind Joe that is just kind of

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:52.630
a falling off now.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.630
Okay.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:54.630
So that's number one.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:59.329
Number two, you'll look at the light blue
and the brown lines on this image that's on

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:00.329
screen.

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:02.169
Those are Bernie and Warren.

00:02:02.169 --> 00:02:04.009
And I'll describe to you what's going on there.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:10.090
Bernie was immediately hurt when Joe Biden
entered the race, and you see that when Biden

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:16.920
hit his high of around 40 plus percent, Bernie
hit his low, but he since recovered and Bernie

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.080
is now pretty solidly in second place, pulling
an average of just over 16%.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.640
Now, Elizabeth Warren, on the other hand,
as I told you a couple of weeks ago, has been

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.640
slowly and steadily growing her support.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.129
And it's now at a campaign high.

00:02:30.129 --> 00:02:35.780
She's moved from being kind of in the 5% area
to seven to 8% in most polls.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:41.010
She's now averaging right about 10% behind
Bernie Sanders, but not drastically.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.010
So.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.109
So let's now look now that we've seen who
the three front runners are, let's look at

00:02:46.109 --> 00:02:49.379
who's done and has no chance the front runners.

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:55.930
Clearly Joe Biden, Bernie Sanders, Elizabeth
Warren behind them, and then there's the second

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:01.579
sort of many tier of candidates, which is
Kahmilah Harrison, Pete, Buddha, judge Harris

00:03:01.579 --> 00:03:09.760
averaging about 7% booted, judge averaging
about 6% now the people who have no chance

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:16.269
and are getting no traction and will likely
start dropping out soon after the debate start

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:22.459
are the folks who are under 1% for sure and
a lot of the people under 2% as well.

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:26.150
Okay, so under 1% and have no chance whatsoever.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:32.260
Tulsi Gabard Bennett Bullock who you may not
even have heard of, Hickenlooper, Seth Moulton,

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:37.900
Bill de Blasio, John Delaney, Kirsten Gillibrand,
Marianne Williamson, Tim Ryan, they're all

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.060
under 1% they just have no chance.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:41.060
Okay?

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.060
I'm not telling them to drop out.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.310
I don't really care one way or the other.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:44.819
They're just non factors.

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:50.379
They're done and I don't even know that there's
that much attention to be paid to their prospects.

00:03:50.379 --> 00:03:53.040
Then you have the one to 2% range.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.040
Okay?

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.959
You've got Andrew Yang there, you have Julio
on Castro, you have Amy Klobuchar who are

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:00.689
between one and 2%.

00:04:00.689 --> 00:04:07.719
They're also realistically done, but if you're
at 2% you can sort of justify staying in a

00:04:07.719 --> 00:04:08.909
little bit longer.

00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:12.939
I know that early I said I liked Andrew Yang
and I still like Andrew Yang.

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:17.560
I know that three months ago he had like a
hypothetical path to the nomination.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.829
If he continued building support.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:21.030
I'm not calling him done now.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:26.990
I mean he's over 1% but if he doesn't get
to like three or 4% after the first debate,

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:32.730
I don't know how he justifies staying in and
then caught in this kind of no man's land

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:40.140
at more than 2% but less than anything significant
are Cory booker at 2.2 and Beto O'rourke at

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:46.620
3.8 I think they're also both done, but that's
enough support to keep raising money.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.289
Keep raising your public profile by, by staying
in.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:51.250
So that's my assessment right now.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:54.320
If you disagree with me on any of this, let
me know politely.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.669
Ideally, if there's someone in the under 1%
area that you think has a shot, tell me who,

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:01.750
